WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.510
Every camera starts at the same spot.

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:03.982
Every depth map is flat.

00:00:04.362 --> 00:00:08.120
A small network nudges them, step after step, until a ring of

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.686
cameras stands around the scene.

00:00:12.082 --> 00:00:13.063
Hi, I'm Lambda.

00:00:13.063 --> 00:00:16.988
Two papers from one lab take away bundle adjustment's two crutches: the

00:00:16.988 --> 00:00:19.278
good first guess, and the point tracks.

00:00:21.283 --> 00:00:25.351
Bundle adjustment moves every camera and every point to fit the pixels.

00:00:25.351 --> 00:00:28.062
Its normal matrix is shaped like an arrow.

00:00:28.482 --> 00:00:32.111
Each point only talks to itself, so its little block is cheap to invert.

00:00:32.882 --> 00:00:34.137
Eliminate the points first.

00:00:34.137 --> 00:00:36.961
What's left is a system in the cameras alone.

00:00:37.682 --> 00:00:40.460
Solve that, then recover each point on its own.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.546
That's every serious solver, and it needs to start close.

00:00:44.883 --> 00:00:48.614
In my flatland toy, with a walk that turns ninety degrees, a

00:00:48.614 --> 00:00:52.033
start five degrees off reaches the minimum in all twenty scenes.

00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:53.588
A cold start, in six.

00:00:55.242 --> 00:00:56.974
TFD-SfM drops the shared point.

00:00:56.974 --> 00:01:01.478
Each side of a match gets its own Gaussian, with its own variance.

00:01:01.858 --> 00:01:05.389
A match that can't be fitted grows its variance and stops pulling.

00:01:05.389 --> 00:01:07.743
As the ring resolves, the two discs merge.

00:01:09.183 --> 00:01:14.566
From identity cameras, classical bundle adjustment gets almost nothing right on ETH3D.

00:01:14.966 --> 00:01:17.429
Gaussian landmarks alone lift it to forty-five.

00:01:17.829 --> 00:01:21.659
Chaining each pose to its parent in a spanning tree adds about thirty points.

00:01:22.059 --> 00:01:25.073
Down-weighting unreliable image pairs takes it to ninety.

00:01:26.408 --> 00:01:30.810
Object scans have a mirror trap: the reflected shape projects almost the same.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:34.196
So it optimises both worlds and keeps the likelier one.

00:01:35.408 --> 00:01:39.483
DeepBA keeps that state, poses and depth maps, and replaces the solver

00:01:39.483 --> 00:01:42.199
with a graph network that predicts each update.

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:45.319
Cameras, image pairs and matches pass messages.

00:01:45.319 --> 00:01:50.407
It sees residuals, never pixels, which is why two million parameters are enough.

00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:55.340
Side by side, the optimiser wins the big scenes, and the network

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.276
wins small bags of tourist photos.

00:01:57.276 --> 00:02:00.180
The protocols differ, so read it as a map.

00:02:01.825 --> 00:02:03.013
And it's fast.

00:02:03.013 --> 00:02:06.972
On two hundred ninety-two images, its solver takes fifty-six seconds.

00:02:06.972 --> 00:02:08.952
COLMAP's takes nine hundred eighty-eight.

00:02:10.242 --> 00:02:14.395
UGG comes from the other side: a video model that also generates

00:02:14.395 --> 00:02:18.202
depth and camera rays, fourteen point eight billion parameters of it.

00:02:18.582 --> 00:02:22.021
Its pose tables leave out the models people actually use.

00:02:23.308 --> 00:02:26.135
A learned cold start, then a classical finish.

00:02:26.135 --> 00:02:27.902
DeepBA's own ablation says so.

00:02:27.902 --> 00:02:30.022
Neither paper has released code yet.

00:02:31.775 --> 00:02:32.738
Thanks for watching!

00:02:36.375 --> 00:02:38.436
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:38.436 --> 00:02:39.025
I'm Lambda.

00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:39.320
Bye!
